<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Persian Socialist Soviet Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1920 CE – 1921 CE · capital Rasht</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived Soviet republic in the Caspian province of Gilan, born from an alliance between the Jangali rebels of Mirza Kuchik Khan and the Bolsheviks.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>From 1915 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-kuchik-khan">Mirza Kuchik Khan</a> led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jangal-movement">Jangali</a> ('forest') movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> against foreign intervention and the weak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> government. In May 1920 a Bolshevik fleet landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandar-e-anzali">Anzali</a> in pursuit of White Russian ships. With Soviet support, Kuchik Khan proclaimed the Persian Socialist Soviet Republic at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasht">Rasht</a> in June 1920.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic was a fragile coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-kuchik-khan">Kuchik Khan's</a> nationalist and religious followers, radical leftists under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ehsanollah-khan-dustdar">Ehsanollah Khan</a> and the new Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, backed by Soviet troops. Disputes over land reform and religion split the leadership within weeks; Kuchik Khan withdrew to the forests, and the communists took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasht">Rasht</a>. A later reconciliation brought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haydar-khan-amu-oghli">Haydar Khan Amu-oghli</a>, who was killed in renewed infighting in 1921.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-persian-treaty-of-friendship-1921">Soviet-Persian Treaty</a> of February 1921, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Soviet Russia</a> withdrew its forces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Khan's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> troops crushed the republic in late 1921, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-kuchik-khan">Kuchik Khan</a> died of exposure in the mountains that December; he is remembered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> as a national hero.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/persian-socialist-soviet-republic.jpg" alt="Persian Socialist Soviet Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Froztbyte · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Persian_Socialist_Soviet_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1920 CE – 1921 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rasht</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mirza Kuchik Khan, Ehsanollah Khan, Haydar Khan Amu-oghli</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Gilaki</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Soviet Republic of Gilan, Gilan Soviet Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=persian-socialist-soviet-republic" aria-label="Persian Socialist Soviet Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5921 836 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Persian Socialist Soviet Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6241 1092L6298 1144"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6298 1144h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6241 1092h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6241 1092h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rasht. <a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=persian-socialist-soviet-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
